# Lab session protocol — when the bench is live

This rule governs what an agent may do when the user says "I have the drone
powered on and the probe attached." Read this every time before acting in
that context.

The full procedure is `docs/lab-runbook.md`. **Skim that file before any
hardware-touching action.**

## Authorization gate

The user must give an explicit authorization sentence of the form:

> "You are authorized to modify code, rebuild, flash, and live-probe the
> running firmware. The drone is disarmed, props are off, and the bench is
> safe. Proceed."

Without that sentence, do not run any write path. Read-only `livewatch read`
becomes available after `tasks.py doctor` is green. Otherwise: stay offline.

Once authorized, the user steps back and the agent decides what to do based
on the task at hand — bug hunting, acceptance testing, calibration sweeps.

## Mandatory pre-flash sequence

Before `tasks.py flash`, run all four checks in order. Any failure → abort.

```bash
.venv/bin/python tasks.py doctor                              # toolchain + probe
.venv/bin/python -m ground_station.livewatch verify           # ELF on disk = on target
.venv/bin/python -m ground_station.livewatch freshness s_ekf.x[3] \
    --samples 5 --delay-ms 20 --require-monotonic             # bridge is fresh
.venv/bin/python -m ground_station.livewatch read DroneStatus.ARM_Status  # DISARMED
```

ARM_Status must equal DISARMED. Any other value (ARMED, FAULT, INIT) is a
hard abort. The user disarms via RC; the agent does not bypass this check.

## Wireless-bridge caveat

If the CMSIS-DAP is wireless (J-Link WiFi, AIRLink, etc.), runs of the same
read can return stale cached values. `livewatch verify` catches stale ELF;
`livewatch freshness` catches stale runtime reads. Re-run freshness after
any long pause and after any value that looks too clean.

The Linux pipeline you built is no different from the Windows one at the
SWD wire layer; both go through pyocd. Wireless vs wired is a probe
hardware choice, not a software one.

## End-of-session report

Before stopping, tell the user:

- what was changed in source
- what was flashed to the target (commit SHA + ELF sha256-16)
- whether the drone is still disarmed (re-read ARM_Status)
- where the new image lives (`firmware/build/JX_FLY.{elf,hex,bin}`)

## Scope discipline (still applies)

Even when authorized to flash, the agent does not:

- arm the vehicle
- spin motors
- write to RAM, peripherals, or registers via livewatch
- bypass the DISARMED check under any pressure

Hardware-safety rules in `.cursor/rules/hardware-safety.mdc` are stricter
than this rule. Where they conflict, the hardware-safety rule wins.